#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 1080.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952} :activity/id #uuid "e36ee21c-371b-5637-8539-624dc138ba57" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106952 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107173 :tutorial/content "Consider an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty, \\infty),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent." :tutorial/id #uuid "b58359c2-9f54-5bd7-8005-df9313559853" :tutorial/math-academy-id 932 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "e2a6a97c-d17d-5e17-9d7b-d0d1dbe224ca" :step/math-academy-id 618349 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106953 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105608 :question/id #uuid "dbd8b706-9ace-5adb-9c8e-29b0d21bfa82" :question/math-academy-id "e-515"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048618} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288010} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351298}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "61313614-0ee3-57e5-8e4b-da016cba1b7b" :step/math-academy-id 618350 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106954 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107099 :tutorial/content "Computing the definite integral of a function over the entire real line requires us to break the integral into two parts and separately evaluate each one.\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x + \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating two improper integrals can be quite time-consuming. However, we can shorten the process if we see that $f(x)$ is an even or odd function. \n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an even function if $f(-x) = f(x).$ Even functions are symmetrical about the $y$-axis. Therefore, if $f(x)$ is even, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = \\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is even and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 2\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\n- Recall that $f(x)$ is an odd function if $f(-x) = -f(x).$ Therefore, if $f(x)$ is odd, we have\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{0} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = -\\int_{0}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nprovided that the integrals are convergent. Moreover, if $f(x)$ is odd and the above integrals are convergent, then\n\n$$\n\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} f(x) \\, \\textrm{d}x = 0.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "04e63304-93d0-5edd-985c-6a732614917f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4359 :tutorial/title "Improper Integrals of Even and Odd Functions"} :step/id #uuid "4ed58d6e-1526-5297-982b-b32247e84e4b" :step/math-academy-id 618353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106955 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105607 :question/id #uuid "705ea272-efb2-54f4-b527-c1d4305e0c6c" :question/math-academy-id "e-514"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106142} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253505} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253507} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288011} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319192} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527936} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535938}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"} :step/id #uuid "647a58a8-d8cb-5567-85af-21033f2a72c6" :step/math-academy-id 618354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106956 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105609 :question/id #uuid "aad39658-8d3f-5893-b7dd-603b62064c5e" :question/math-academy-id "e-516"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d083b53b-8c39-5785-a250-5649001e822b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048946}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106282} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106492} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253521} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332946} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549022} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549058}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Area of an Unbounded Region"} :step/id #uuid "f45b6067-3889-5762-becb-da9ab2269834" :step/math-academy-id 618357}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186106007}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536052} :activity/id #uuid "320827af-0236-5baf-ad26-fd203d4a3819" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536007 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253506 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f1b0944-5746-51e3-95af-0c78d353b0b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251078} {:answer/id #uuid "5483cc68-a72b-52f6-99ad-108af7c29b48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251245} {:answer/id #uuid "712f686d-666e-5b50-837d-069f1c8844f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251478} {:answer/id #uuid "831c3d8d-8d9b-5446-847d-4a9578ff7cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} {:answer/id #uuid "da79cc7e-e889-5625-9d36-6e8c5b69befe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252473}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186251659} :answer-field/id #uuid "6264318d-7488-52ce-83d2-d05d6144f903" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252983}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "64a6785a-7bbf-5956-bb67-37d306305466" :question/math-academy-id "q-118776" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0727a7ae-2a4f-5a1a-b263-107fab74a519" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536013}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536013 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d3be81-fa17-5678-892f-649528299dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319449} {:answer/id #uuid "ba22ce2a-0962-5777-b986-98a5bafb7d11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535718}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535718} :answer-field/id #uuid "efd6a557-0dd5-5de4-9841-579dcad1aa36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320665}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "5cdd21e7-31ed-571d-8af2-2513392df808" :question/math-academy-id "q-351605" :question/problem "Find the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ and the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch a graph of the region.\n\n![](images/2b/2b7b85dd8894c73275816a8ee08a8c8a41fb41a327d20ed7bedc489f7e2e55ff.png)\n\nWe find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{2}$, then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+9}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{3}))+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0)-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3})) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{|x|}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "20ef6a64-43e5-569e-9232-97b79c35df56"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535938 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19e74ed4-4bf4-58a5-82f1-bb5ffa3a7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535474} {:answer/id #uuid "4bbbf7a3-60c8-55b3-8fe4-dad2f3bdc622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} {:answer/id #uuid "6899860b-81ea-5720-98fc-80926761f0f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535599} {:answer/id #uuid "ceb453c5-9589-5071-99e3-645a2efd0834" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535747} {:answer/id #uuid "e03abd1a-722a-5b07-a324-580b73ea930e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "The integral is divergent" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535780}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535545} :answer-field/id #uuid "da97b470-1a5e-578a-80de-9d4315049c8f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535913}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "21f7ba2a-9a0f-57d4-9aff-030342b38301" :question/math-academy-id "q-86453" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the second of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=1+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{2}{{u}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{4x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }({-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}|}_{0}^{a}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(-\\frac{1}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{(1+0)^{2}}) \\\\  & =-0+1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4|x|}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "6cb888af-c468-5ec7-990d-1e6270a76de3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536007}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536043 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186288012 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ed6a20c-d3bb-592f-8e4f-a620b4dc19e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380} {:answer/id #uuid "75e76cf0-383c-5fca-99ea-28387d4e5368" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186285887} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc4a679-5371-540b-a4ee-e82ab4ebc0e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286068} {:answer/id #uuid "95355a0d-4348-58b2-ac15-b501e96ac9a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286144} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a5c260-a436-5bf1-aa3a-179f0a2476b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186286916}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186285380} :answer-field/id #uuid "e01f0e05-7912-5f4f-a1e6-e67a3934581a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186287604}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9b5dd8ac-fb74-5814-828f-f32767c78b1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-20797" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{({e}^{{x}^{3}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "cd28a652-1df4-5863-b66e-4a60f9e2ee7f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536052 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332946 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4fbbcb6c-2e6a-5a12-9d2e-23b23de5892e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330675} {:answer/id #uuid "6400598e-ce3c-555e-ba3f-3969e7f485d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330838} {:answer/id #uuid "703491e6-04b7-509f-b24e-1423bfa7f4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330956} {:answer/id #uuid "c1d34bc7-ffea-5420-9a4b-671380a6ae98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331673} {:answer/id #uuid "cecf1f59-e403-5aa0-a3f9-39fb9dc1ad4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186330956} :answer-field/id #uuid "ebe4f3fe-a5f0-5d9d-abc1-a06051457234" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332614}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "55c54cd7-0604-5984-8b9b-055f20084d5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-5966" :question/problem "![](images/dd/dd286aa5384dae22d8fff4bb29799078547064926697d71b0b0ca8739641408f.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{6}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{x}{6}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{a}{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{36+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fd9a0382-9f34-5402-8fbe-b792883b94da" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536043}}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535428}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046645} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549074} :activity/id #uuid "eed14073-dc62-5f83-88ec-a63140fff982" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048880 :topic/id #uuid "c9449775-49cd-5723-869d-965280e34916" :topic/math-academy-id 1382} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549074 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549058 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d2be98-9a4d-5c88-abaa-0360571b0ab6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548436} {:answer/id #uuid "41c64781-3167-56a3-b3e8-fd75bb289c1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548480} {:answer/id #uuid "61db2b55-0411-5c2c-8bcf-dbb5a9ead9bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548540} {:answer/id #uuid "7b7f8da8-6744-55ff-b218-c7923a9889bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548584} {:answer/id #uuid "e3396cf7-276b-5255-a8b9-c8f3c0ee558c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548795}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548795} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae057d8d-edb0-54d6-852b-f3d8185d31f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "e38cbfdc-40f4-5e23-81ff-d9532344f653" :question/math-academy-id "q-118856" :question/problem "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u=-{x}^{4}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}{x}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-{x}^{4}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{4}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x{|}^{3}{e}^{-{x}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "06d66274-5a32-576e-a52b-391edbc866d9" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549089}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320868 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c78b4a2-6387-513f-b43f-e6d54aec13ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319809} {:answer/id #uuid "0a011c53-bc00-5286-8ae6-e77021b09c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548373} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab5f805c-e05b-5b52-9538-96672619b94f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aaf1e23c-5c2a-568b-9798-e760d6ad39ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-351545" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{9+4{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0b341115-0cca-598f-b097-bbbf9d6cbc06" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549022 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d04d6f59-7d03-5768-bf23-8c1a24199af9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548763}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548763} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba5aae1a-3e45-5846-af9a-9b9e31b42dfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548947}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "86d71eff-689a-54e2-8c09-3fae339805fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-351589" :question/problem "![](images/f7/f78efc78b9f7cded50b29ba63855d81ff4e9ee8b7d12aa9673fac1cb7644e50a.png)\n\nFind the total area $A$ of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}5{e}^{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{x+1}{|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0+1}-{e}^{a+1}) \\\\  & =5(e-0) \\\\  & =5e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $5{e}^{-|x|+1}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x=5e.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }5{e}^{-|x|+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =5e+5e \\\\  & =10e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1d167963-e125-5607-90e6-742e213a968e" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549115}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549089 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319189 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30cd0d9b-5fe9-51b3-9519-02b039db9514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317715} :answer-field/id #uuid "89037d38-162e-5bd4-86ef-575cf06fd386" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318723}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "347dab61-bb37-50f6-ab92-42466a2565f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-347615" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25} & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{(x/5)^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}\\cdot 5{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{5})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{5})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+25}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}+\\frac{3\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "386ac35b-932b-5c6b-b512-8af34540aa39" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549077}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549115 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0348eb59-0243-542c-b9af-f817342c77ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319403} {:answer/id #uuid "d16a7ece-3c28-5cdc-b1c9-a82826a0c636" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548765}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548765} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e8c31ba-b28d-5c04-adfd-7b5f53d9b579" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "85d0c74e-4503-59dc-b9ab-8cb0d6da3ead" :question/math-academy-id "q-351595" :question/problem "![](images/cc/ccd0312a25669fec949475eaadb042e0c660d094c7579f5bc4f98b68fc9be78d.png)\n\nFind the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis, as shown above. Do not round the answer.\n\n$A=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{2}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{2}){|}_{a}^{0}\\,) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{4+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{3\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "807ac06a-e2d7-58a6-a284-e84be5d574ae"}] :activity/title "Improper Integrals Over the Real Line" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186548357}]}
